      ['Because a 95% confidence level is simply too low, and only a 99% figure can ever be trusted by a risk team','Because parametric VaR assumes a normal distribution, but real returns have fat tails, extreme losses occur more often than the model predicts','Because VaR has to be recalculated weekly rather than daily, and a daily figure is always an understatement','Because $8 million is too round a number, and a VaR result ending in zeros is a sign the math was rushed'],
      1,
      'Correct! The parametric method\'s assumption of normally distributed returns is its Achilles\' heel. Real market returns exhibit fat tails, extreme events like the 2008 crash happen far more often than the bell curve predicts. This means parametric VaR consistently underestimates tail risk.',
      'Not quite. The issue is the normal distribution assumption. Real market returns have "fat tails", extreme losses occur much more frequently than a normal distribution would predict. The parametric method, by assuming normality, systematically underestimates the probability and severity of extreme events.'
