        'Credit rating agencies, Moody\'s, S&P, and Fitch, are the gatekeepers of the bond market. Their ratings determine borrowing costs: a AAA rating means near-zero default risk and the lowest interest rates, while a CCC rating signals severe distress and sky-high borrowing costs. The difference between investment grade (BBB and above) and junk (BB and below) is a critical threshold, many institutional investors are prohibited from holding junk-rated bonds.',
        'The agencies catastrophically failed in the 2008 crisis by assigning AAA ratings to mortgage-backed securities that were actually filled with toxic subprime loans. They used flawed models that assumed housing prices would never decline nationally. When housing crashed, 91% of AAA-rated subprime MBS issued in 2006 were eventually downgraded to junk status. The agencies earned fees from the same banks whose products they rated, a massive conflict of interest.',
