    cq('A bank with $100 billion in assets holds primarily long-term bonds purchased at low rates. Interest rates have risen sharply, creating unrealized losses. Why does the CFO\'s argument to "just hold to maturity" potentially fail?',
      ['Because long-term bonds almost always default before maturity, so the held-to-maturity plan rarely reaches its payoff','Because unrealized losses become realized if depositors withdraw and the bank must sell bonds to meet withdrawals, exactly what happened to SVB','Because regulators will always step in and force a bank to sell its bonds well before they reach their maturity date','Because interest rates can only ever rise from here, so the unrealized losses on the bond book can never recover'],
      1,'"Hold to maturity" assumes the bank won\'t be forced to sell. But if depositors withdraw (as SVB experienced), the bank must sell bonds to generate cash, converting unrealized losses into actual losses. SVB planned to hold $91B in bonds to maturity but was forced to sell $21B at a $1.8B loss when deposits fled. The "hold to maturity" strategy only works when you control the timing, and during a run, you don\'t.'),
    cq('Which combination of factors makes a bank most vulnerable to a rapid liquidity crisis in the social media age?',
      ['High profitability, diversified deposits, and short-duration assets, which together would actually steady a bank in a panic','High uninsured deposit ratio, concentrated depositor base connected through social media, and long-duration assets with unrealized losses','Low profitability on its own, since a bank that earns thin margins is the single thing that triggers a depositor stampede','High employee turnover on its own, since losing staff is what causes depositors to lose confidence and pull their money'],
      1,'SVB demonstrated the deadly combination: (1) 94% uninsured deposits meant depositors had maximum incentive to withdraw, (2) tech-concentrated depositors communicated instantly, coordinating the run in hours, and (3) long-duration bonds with massive unrealized losses meant selling assets to meet withdrawals would crystallize catastrophic losses. All three must be addressed together, fixing just one is insufficient.')
