      ['What is the "insurance paradox" of hedging?','Protection is cheapest when markets are calm and nobody thinks they need it. It becomes most expensive during panics when everyone wants it simultaneously. Goldman Sachs bought hedges in calm 2006, by 2008, the same protection cost 5-10x more.'],
      ['How did collars help Goldman Sachs survive 2008?','Goldman bought puts and sold calls on mortgage exposure in 2006, creating cheap or zero-cost collars. When mortgage markets collapsed, the puts generated billions in gains. The collar strategy was key to Goldman earning $3.7 billion in 2007 while competitors lost tens of billions.'],
      ['What is "delta hedging"?','A technique where you continuously adjust the amount of the underlying asset you hold to offset changes in your options positions. Market makers delta hedge to remain neutral to price direction, profiting only from the spread between buying and selling options.']
