      1,
      'Correct! A 3% leverage ratio means capital must be at least 3% of total assets, so max leverage is 100/3 = approximately 33:1. While this seems similar to Lehman\'s 31:1, the leverage ratio is a backstop. In practice, risk-weighted capital requirements (CET1 of 7%+) are far more binding, pushing effective leverage much lower. JPMorgan typically operates at about 10:1.',
      'Think about it mathematically: a 3% leverage ratio means capital/assets >= 3%, so assets/capital <= 33:1. But this is just the backstop ratio. The risk-weighted CET1 requirements (7%+) are more restrictive in practice, keeping well-run banks at 8-12:1 leverage.'
