        { term: 'Risk-Weighted Assets (RWA)', definition: 'A bank\'s assets weighted by their risk level. Cash has a 0% weight, government bonds 0-20%, mortgages 35-50%, and corporate loans 100%. Capital ratios are calculated against RWA, not total assets.', example: 'A bank with $100B in Treasuries (0% weight) and $100B in corporate loans (100% weight) has RWA of $100B, not $200B.' },
        { term: 'Countercyclical Capital Buffer', definition: 'An additional capital buffer of 0-2.5% that regulators can activate during credit booms to build extra cushion. It\'s released during downturns to support lending.', example: 'In 2020, regulators released the countercyclical buffer so banks could keep lending through the COVID crisis without breaching capital minimums.' },
        { term: 'G-SIB (Global Systemically Important Bank)', definition: 'A bank designated as so large and interconnected that its failure could threaten the entire global financial system. G-SIBs face additional capital surcharges of 1-3.5%.', example: 'JPMorgan, Bank of America, Citigroup, and Goldman Sachs are U.S. G-SIBs. JPMorgan has the highest surcharge at 3.5% due to its size and interconnectedness.' }
